<!--
  PHPMD baseline — regenerated 2026-08-02 when `composer phpmd` stopped being a
  no-op. Until this commit the script ended in

      || echo 'PHPMD reported violations beyond the baseline — ... NOT yet a hard gate.'

  so it ALWAYS exited 0. It was not merely unenforced locally: the shared CI
  workflow (ConductionNL/.github .github/workflows/quality.yml) runs
  `composer phpmd`, so the `|| echo` made the "PHP Quality (phpmd)" job report
  SUCCESS while printing violations — e.g. run 30760172028 (2026-08-02), green
  with dozens of CyclomaticComplexity findings in its log.

  WHY THESE ENTRIES ARE BASELINED RATHER THAN FIXED
  Measured on this tree, in nextcloud:32-apache (PHP 8.3.32): phpmd reported 76
  violations past the previous baseline (82 with no baseline at all). Every one
  of them is a complexity- or naming-class rule:

      CyclomaticComplexity, NpathComplexity, WeightedMethodCount,
      ElseExpression, CouplingBetweenObjects, LongMethod, LongClass,
      BooleanArgumentFlag, LongVariable, ShortVariable, TooManyMethods,
      StaticAccess, UnusedFormalParameter, LongParameterList, MissingImport,
      LongClassName, TooManyPublicMethods

  None of these asserts incorrect behaviour; they assert that a method is long
  or branchy. Acting on them means restructuring 48 files, which is a refactor
  with real regression risk and no defect to point at — it does not belong in a
  change whose purpose is to make the gate report the truth.

  The correctness tools are separately clean on this same tree, which is why
  baselining the mess-detector findings is not hiding anything:
      phpcs   0 findings, exit 0
      psalm   0 findings, exit 0  (errorLevel 4, findUnusedCode enabled)
      phpstan 0 findings, exit 0  (level 5)

  From this commit on the baseline is a RATCHET: any NEW phpmd violation fails
  `composer phpmd`, and therefore `composer check:strict` and CI. Burning this
  file down is tracked separately; do not regenerate it to silence a new finding.
-->
